# Arguments for the wash-login service front door. Installed as
# /etc/default/wash-login (systemd, sourced via the unit's EnvironmentFile) and
# as /etc/conf.d/wash-login (OpenRC, sourced automatically by the init script).
#
# wash-login binds 0.0.0.0:10000 by design: it is a NETWORK service (see
# docs/MULTIUSER.md). For any non-loopback bind you must declare a cookie
# policy, or wash-login refuses to start rather than leak credentials:
#
#   --cookie-secure         Sets the Secure flag on session cookies. Use this
#                           when a TLS terminator (reverse proxy) sits in front
#                           so the browser reaches wash over HTTPS. RECOMMENDED
#                           for anything exposed beyond a trusted LAN.
#
#   --allow-insecure-cookie Permit plain-HTTP login (no TLS in front). Ships
#                           passwords and session cookies in the clear — only
#                           acceptable on a network you fully trust.
#
# The shipped default allows plain-HTTP login so a fresh install is reachable
# and usable out of the box. Put wash behind TLS and switch to --cookie-secure
# for any exposed deployment. Other useful flags: --listen host:port,
# --max-sessions-per-uid N, --cookie-ttl DUR (see `wash-login --help`).
WASH_LOGIN_ARGS="--allow-insecure-cookie"
